**TL;DR:** `Self` (3.11, PEP 673) means 'the type of `self`/`cls`' without a boilerplate TypeVar; it preserves subclass types through chaining.

<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="640" height="120" role="img"><rect width="640" height="120" rx="12" fill="#0f172a"/><rect x="20" y="20" width="280" height="80" rx="10" fill="#3730a3"/><rect x="340" y="20" width="280" height="80" rx="10" fill="#14532d"/><text x="160" y="65" text-anchor="middle" fill="white" font-family="monospace" font-size="14">TypeVar boilerplate</text><text x="480" y="65" text-anchor="middle" fill="white" font-family="monospace" font-size="14">Self: one word</text></svg>

| | TypeVar pattern | Self |
|---|---|---|
| Declaration | `T = TypeVar('T', bound='Base')` + `def clone(self: T) -> T` | `def clone(self) -> Self` |
| Subclass return | Works but verbose | Works, concise |
| Classmethod | `def create(cls: type[T]) -> T` | `def create(cls) -> Self` |
| Readability | Noisy | Idiomatic 3.11+ |

Use `Self` for any method returning `self`, `cls`, or a copy — it keeps `Subclass.method()` typed as `Subclass`, not `Base`.

> **Interview tip:** Default answer: `from typing import Self` and `def with_name(self, n: str) -> Self: ... return self` — no TypeVar needed.

```python
from typing import Self

class Animal:
    def __init__(self, name: str): self.name = name
    def with_name(self, name: str) -> Self:
        return type(self)(name)
    @classmethod
    def create(cls) -> Self:
        return cls("default")

class Dog(Animal):
    def bark(self): print(f"{self.name} woof")

d = Dog("Rex").with_name("Max")  # typed as Dog, not Animal
print(type(d).__name__, d.name)
d.bark()
print(Dog.create().name)
```
